# Adding your own steps to wiretap-server-ui's install

**This file is owned by wslib-appliance-xtask and is rewritten by every
`packaging render`.** So is every maintainer script beside it. Do not edit those
— put your product's own steps in the hook directories described below, which
wslib-appliance-xtask never writes.

## The two directories

`postinst` and `prerm` each run `run-parts` over one directory, if it exists.
`make-deb.sh` ships your scripts there from the directory `hooks_dir` names in
`appliance.toml` — `<hooks_dir>/postinst.d/*` and `<hooks_dir>/prerm.d/*`, each
`0755`, checked before the build for the two ways a hook silently never runs
(below). Anything else in that directory is refused, because nothing else there
could ever run.

| Script | Hook directory | Runs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `postinst` | `/usr/share/wiretap-server-ui/postinst.d/` | on `configure`, after the account and the configuration directory exist, and **before** the unit is started or restarted |
| `prerm` | `/usr/share/wiretap-server-ui/prerm.d/` | on `remove`, before the daemon is stopped; and on `upgrade` and `deconfigure`, where nothing is stopped at all |

## What is not a hook

Three things a product ships are files rather than steps, and `make-deb.sh`
ships them without a hook:

- **A unit drop-in.** Every `*.conf` in `scaffold/systemd/wiretap-server-ui.service.d/`
  goes to `/usr/lib/systemd/system/wiretap-server-ui.service.d/`. The `.sample` beside
  them does not — see it for what a drop-in can and cannot change.
- **A file under `/etc` or `/usr/share/wiretap-server-ui`**, named in `[package.files]`
  as `"/destination" = "source/in/the/repo"`, shipped `0644`. One under `/etc` is
  a conffile: dpkg keeps an operator's edit across an upgrade and asks before
  replacing one the upgrade changed. `/etc/wiretap-server-ui/config.toml` is refused
  there — the package ships that one itself; ship a `config.toml.example`
  beside it.
- **`scaffold/debian/copyright`**, a DEP-5 file you write, shipped to
  `/usr/share/doc/<package>/` (`[targets.deb] package`, or `name`) beside a
  changelog the build makes from its version.

## A hook that edits another daemon's configuration

The shape, for a drop-in some other daemon reads: write it, ask that daemon
whether the result parses, and take it back out with a warning rather than fail
when it does not — a refused install is the wrong answer to a configuration
problem an operator can fix:

```sh
#!/bin/sh
set -e
case "$1" in
configure)
    DROPIN=/etc/otherd/conf.d/50-wiretap-server-ui.conf
    if command -v otherd >/dev/null 2>&1 && [ -d /etc/otherd/conf.d ]; then
        # Written in place, because a check mode reads the live configuration
        # and its include glob — a file under another name is not what it checks.
        printf 'Setting value\n' > "$DROPIN"
        # No running systemd means a chroot, where a check that wants the box's
        # own state cannot run: leave the file for the first real boot.
        if [ -d /run/systemd/system ] && ! otherd --check 2>/dev/null; then
            rm -f "$DROPIN"
            echo "wiretap-server-ui: warning: otherd refused $DROPIN, so it was removed" >&2
        fi
    fi
    ;;
esac
```

That file is *written* by the hook rather than shipped through `[package.files]`
because its worth depends on a check only a running box can make. A file that is
right unconditionally — a `config.toml.example` — is a shipped file, and the
conffile handling comes free.

**There is no `postrm` hook, and that is not an oversight.** Debian Policy 6.5
has `postrm` run *after* the package's files have been removed — so a script
shipped in `/usr/share/wiretap-server-ui/postrm.d/` is already deleted by the time
`postrm` could call it. `prerm` is the last window your code can run in. If you
need to take data with you before a purge, take it there.

`run-parts` runs the scripts in lexical order, so name them `10-`, `20-` and so
on. Two ways a hook silently never runs, both worth knowing before you debug one:

- **It must be executable.** `run-parts` skips anything without the bit set, and
  says nothing.
- **Its name must not contain a dot.** The default name rule is
  `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` only, so the `.sh` suffix that feels natural is the usual way
  a hook is ignored.

## What a hook is given

The maintainer script's first two arguments, passed through. So a `postinst`
hook gets `configure` and, on an upgrade, the version being upgraded from:

```sh
#!/bin/sh
set -e
case "$1" in
configure)
    if [ -z "$2" ]; then
        echo "no earlier version of this package"
    else
        echo "upgrading from $2"
    fi
    ;;
esac
```

**An empty `$2` does not mean nothing came before.** A switch between this
appliance's packages configures the incoming one with an empty `$2` over the
configuration and state the other one left, so a hook that sets something up
once tests what is on disk. Likewise a `prerm` hook's `remove` may be one
package of this appliance making way for another.

dpkg passes more than two in some forms (`prerm remove in-favour <pkg> <ver>`).
Those are not forwarded.

## A failing hook fails the operation

Every site passes `--exit-on-error`, and the maintainer scripts run under
`set -e`. So a hook that exits non-zero stops the run there and leaves dpkg
reporting the operation as failed — a failed configure for a `postinst` hook, a
failed removal for a `prerm` one.

That is deliberate. Without it `run-parts` carries on, which for a schema
migration means the second script running against what the first did not finish.
**A refused upgrade is recoverable; a half-migrated appliance in the field is
somebody driving to it.**

The consequence to design for: a hook must be safe to run again, because the
operator's next move after fixing the cause is `dpkg --configure -a`.

## Do not migrate the database from a hook

The obvious use for a `postinst` hook is a schema migration, and it is the wrong
one. `prerm` deliberately does not stop the daemon on an upgrade — so a
`postinst` hook runs while the **old** binary still has the database open, and
`postinst` runs the hooks before it restarts anything.

Your schema belongs in your own `sqlx` migration chain, which `Store::open`
composes with the platform's and runs inside the daemon at startup. That is the
one place where nothing else has the file open. Hooks are for what lives outside
the database: a directory to create, a file from an older deployment to convert,
a service to poke.

## What the maintainer scripts already do, so you do not

- Creates `wiretap-server-ui` and the `wiretap-server-ui` group.
- Creates `/etc/wiretap-server-ui` and sets it to `root:wiretap-server-ui` mode `0751`. systemd
  creates the directory too and applies that mode — what it will **not** do is
  set the group, and without that an unprivileged daemon cannot read its own
  configuration.
- Enables and starts the unit, and restarts rather than stops-and-starts on an
  upgrade.
- On purge only, removes `/var/lib/wiretap-server-ui` and `/etc/wiretap-server-ui` and the account.
  A purge, here and above, removes nothing while another package of this
  appliance is on the box, and fails, to be run again, when `dpkg-query` cannot
  say whether one is.

**It ships `/etc/wiretap-server-ui/config.toml` as a conffile**, with every listener
and `[tls]` key in it commented. The package's own values, `[targets.deb]`'s
`bind_address` and `https_port`, are in `/usr/share/wiretap-server-ui/config.toml`,
which is not a conffile and is replaced on every upgrade; the unit names it in
`<PREFIX>_PACKAGE_CONFIG` and sets no listener `Environment=`, so a key
uncommented in the conffile is where a port moves. `redirect_port` defaults to
`0`: no plain-HTTP listener unless an operator asks for one. dpkg keeps an
operator's edit across an upgrade, and asks before replacing an edited file
that the upgrade changed. It unpacks `0640`, and `postinst` takes away any
world access on every configure and sets group `wiretap-server-ui` only from `root`,
leaving a stricter mode, an operator's group and a symlink alone. A kept
`config.toml` the daemon's user cannot read stops the unit at start, as
before. An operator may delete it: the package's file and the defaults answer
every setting. An upgrade that ships it unchanged leaves it deleted; one that
changed it asks, keeping it deleted by default (`--force-confold` does so
without asking); `dpkg -i --force-confmiss` puts it back.

It does **not** generate a TLS certificate, and it must not: a package
installed into a chroot while an image is assembled would make one private key
shared by every box written from that image, named after the build host.

**The daemon makes its own instead**, at first start, when it finds neither
`cert.pem` nor `key.pem`. Those default to `/var/lib/wiretap-server-ui/` rather than
`/etc/wiretap-server-ui/`, because this directory is `root:wiretap-server-ui` mode `0751` — an
unprivileged daemon can read it and cannot write it, and widening that would let
everyone in the `wiretap-server-ui` group rewrite the daemon's configuration.

To supply your own, uncomment `[tls]` in `/etc/wiretap-server-ui/config.toml` and name
them; put both files in `/etc/wiretap-server-ui/`, owned `root:wiretap-server-ui` with the key
`0640`. With both present nothing is generated. **The directory lets every
account traverse it**, so a file in here is as private as its own mode; the
daemon warns at start when `config.toml` is readable by everyone. **Supply
both or neither** — a pair you named that is missing a half, or will not load,
refuses to start rather than being moved aside and replaced, which is what the
daemon does to a pair of its own.
